<h2>When does reactivation make sense?</h2><p>Reactivation may fit a practice with a usable existing contact list and capacity to respond. It is different from advertising to people who have never contacted the office. A new practice without an established patient list may need to prioritize acquisition first.</p><p>Patients stop visiting for many reasons: a completed care plan, a move, scheduling constraints, or a change in preferences. Outreach should respect those differences. It should not assume that every person needs more treatment.</p>
<h2>How should a campaign work?</h2><ol><li><strong>Review the audience.</strong> Have the practice approve who is eligible, which contact channels may be used, and which records must be excluded.</li><li><strong>Choose a useful invitation.</strong> Write a clear reason to reconnect, with accurate terms and a simple reply or booking step.</li><li><strong>Prepare the handoff.</strong> Assign staff to handle questions, appointment requests, and contact preferences.</li><li><strong>Launch and review.</strong> Monitor delivery, replies, and exceptions before deciding whether further outreach is appropriate.</li><li><strong>Reconcile outcomes.</strong> Compare campaign activity with booking and attendance records for the same window.</li></ol><p>ChiroCandy can coordinate campaign strategy, copy, email, and follow-up with the practice. Confirm channels, list preparation, staff responsibilities, and costs in your scope. Use our <a href="/blog/reactivation-campaign-ideas/">reactivation campaign guide</a> for audience and message planning.</p>
<h2>What should you measure?</h2><ul><li><strong>Eligible contacts:</strong> the reviewed audience, excluding suppressed or inappropriate records.</li><li><strong>Delivered messages and replies:</strong> keep channel activity separate from unique people.</li><li><strong>Confirmed bookings:</strong> appointments recorded in the scheduling system.</li><li><strong>Attended return visits:</strong> attendance confirmed by the practice.</li><li><strong>Costs and collections:</strong> use the same campaign cohort and stated reporting window.</li></ul><p>Cost per attended return visit is included campaign cost divided by confirmed attended return visits. State which costs are included. If there are no attended visits or attendance is unavailable, do not present a cost-per-visit result.</p>
<h2>What evidence should you request?</h2><p>Ask for a dated, permission-cleared campaign example with audience size, outreach channel, spending, replies, bookings, and attendance. A lead-generation case is useful context but does not prove a reactivation rate. Our <a href="/case-studies/">case-study library</a> identifies the source and limits of its available results; the <a href="/blog/evaluate-chiropractic-marketing-case-studies/">evidence guide</a> helps you evaluate a proposed example.</p>
<h2>How does it fit the wider plan?</h2><p><a href="/get-new-patients/">New-patient acquisition</a> brings new inquiries into the practice. Reactivation addresses an eligible existing audience. <a href="/services/ai-patient-coordinator/">AI CA</a> and <a href="/services/voice-ai/">Voice AI</a> can support agreed follow-up workflows, with staff responsible for exceptions and care decisions.</p><p><a class="btn btn--primary" href="/schedule/">Discuss a reactivation campaign →</a></p>
</ServiceLayout>
